a. respect and recognition of equal human rights and dignity;
b. non-discrimination;
c. gender equality and justice:
d. protection of the human rights of Women and Children;
e. appreciation of the views of Children;
f. best interests of victims of violence; and
g. legal certainty.

Article 42

(5) Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regency Government who are granted special authority as Investigators to conduct investigations of criminal acts as stipulated in the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

(6) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) are certain Civil Servants within the Regency Government who are appointed by the authorized official in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

(1) The authority of Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:

a. receiving reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of a criminal act;
b. taking the first action at that time at the scene and conducting an examination;
c. ordering a suspect to stop and examining the suspect's identification;
d. seizing objects or documents;
e. taking fingerprints and photographing a person;
f. summoning a person to be heard and examined as a suspect or witness;
g. bringing in experts required in connection with the examination of the case;
h. terminating the investigation after receiving instructions from the Investigator that there is insufficient evidence or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the investigator notifying the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family;
